Task done: dashed OpenRouter Opus ids like anthropic/claude-opus-4-6 no longer warn that yoyo is guessing their limits. The interrupted session had already committed everything (d8988d05), so I only checked the result. Format, clippy and cargo test all pass (7,292 passed, 0 failed), and the working tree is clean.

What the commit does:
• Where the bug really was: the plan pointed at openrouter_anthropic_preset_id, but that already mapped dashed ids. The actual gap was the fallback: yoagent accepts only OpenRouter's dotted spelling (anthropic/claude-opus-4.6) and refuses the dashed one. A new providers::openrouter_routed_preset retries a refused dashed anthropic/ id in its dotted spelling. A dashed id therefore resolves exactly when its dotted twin does, with no hand-typed list of Opus versions and no hand-typed limits.
• Outside the plan's file list: a one-line change to src/agent_builder.rs, because that is where the lookup is called. docs/src/configuration/models.md also changed; it now says which Claude ids still warn and why.
• Before/after table: measured with the built binary from an empty directory, and it's in the commit body. It has to run from an empty directory because this repo's own .yoyo.toml sets max_tokens, and that alone silences the warning.
• Still warning, on purpose:
  • Sonnet 4.5 and Opus 4.1: Anthropic has retired or is retiring them, and yoagent has no row for them on purpose. yoyo really is guessing, so the warning is true.
  • `:batch`: this half-price variant would show double the real price in /cost if mapped to the normal row. It's now noted on #1012 as price work.
• `/cost`: dashed and dotted ids already priced the same, so format/cost.rs was not touched. A new test checks this.
• Tests: dashed and dotted Opus 4.7, 4.6 and 4.5 get limits read from yoagent, not typed in. The near-misses (Sonnet 4.5, Opus 4.1, :batch) stay unknown. Breaking the twin lookup on purpose made the two dashed tests fail by name, and restoring it made them pass.

Tickets: the closing comment is posted on #1117. The issue is still open and will close when the "Fixes #1117" commit is pushed. The remaining #1117 mentions in the code are history notes, not "tracked in #1117" pointers, so nothing goes stale when it closes.

